# Summon one human east of the player and beat it to death. (bd inc-q98a)
#
# Not a session of its own: it starts in play mode with wizard mode already
# entered, and tools/keys/monster-memory-known.keys @includes it ten times.
# It is a file rather than ten copies because ten copies drift.
#
# THE WIZARD MENU IS OPENED BY @until AND NOT BY A BARE w. A long message --
# the anger line a kill prints, wrapped over two lines -- can take the next
# keystroke to dismiss itself, and a w spent there leaves the menu shut and
# every later key in this block against the wrong screen. Measured: the ninth
# summon of ten lost its w that way. Pressing w until the menu's own text is
# on screen costs nothing when the first one lands.
#
# THE FIRST BLOW NEEDS A YES. A summoned human is created peaceful, so the
# first strike asks "Confirm attack the human? [yn]". It asks once: the blow
# makes the human hostile, and no later blow on the same one is questioned.
# Each summon is a new peaceful human, so the yes belongs here and not in the
# caller.
#
# THE LOOP TESTS "h human", the sidebar's "Things in View" entry for the live
# creature, which sits above the corpses because creatures sort first. @while
# tests before each pass, so the loop stops on the blow that kills and the
# player never steps east onto the corpse -- which is what keeps him adjacent
# to the square the next summon uses.

@until "Monster Summoning" w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Humans"
@choose "human"
RIGHT
ENTER

RIGHT
@while "Confirm attack" y
@until "killing the" RIGHT
